  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nOnshore flow from overnight has lead to some areas of fog at the \nhead of the Lake and into portions of the NW WI. This fog will \nquickly erode after sunrise. Surface high pressure will be moving \nacross the region today providing us with mostly sunny skies and \nhighs in the upper 70s to low 80s. Winds will also be shifting from \nout of the east to out of the south, the exception will be areas \nclose to Lake Superior where onshore flow will continue and keep \nthings cooler.\n\nMonday/Tuesday:\n\nHigh pressure gets shunted off to the southeast on Monday allowing \nfor broad southerly winds and warm air advection to take hold of the \nregion. During the morning hours a strong low level jet is expected \nto develop across the Northern Plains which will increase the \nchances of some elevated storms to develop in the Red River Valley. \nA couple hundred J/kg of MUCAPE with a bulk shear of 40-50kts could \nlead to severe storms that generate large hail. The current suite of \nhigh res guidance favors this being farther west outside of our CWA \nwhere the HREF is highlighting some swaths of higher updraft \nhelicity. There is a fair amount of uncertainty on whether this \nonset of morning storms can hold together as they march into NE MN. \nHowever, if they do hold together the convective parameter space \nlooks less favorable for strong storms in our CWA and may end up\nbeing light showers.\n\nActivity is expected to ramp back up in the afternoon and evening. \nDespite the morning storm activity, the region will see ample \nrecovery with southerly flow promoting warm advection and pushing \nMLCAPE upwards of 1000-2000 J/kg. A cold front will move east \nthrough the late afternoon and evening period triggering convection. \nBulk shear will still be bountiful with an upper level jet rounding \nand upper level low moving into Manitoba. Supercells will be \npossible capable of producing large hail and damaging winds. A \ntornado can't be ruled out at this time, but the better streamwise \nvorticity looks to be over the Red River Valley, which then fades as \nthe storm mode becomes more linear as it tracks into our CWA. \nShowers and storms will continue to move across the region through \nthe overnight period with the severe risk fading in the early \nmorning hours. Thereafter, showers and a few weaker storms will \ncontinue through the region Tuesday as the cold front pushes west to \neast. \n\nMid week:\n\nHigh pressure over the region for Wednesday temperatures staying in \nthe 70s. Temperatures will slowly increase through the end of the \nwork week with intermittent rain chances possible as the upper level \npattern turns more zonal.",
